  <h1>DeepInfer open-sourced <span class="accent">— what DSH / V4.1-Flash installers should know</span></h1>
  <p class="muted small" style="margin-bottom:20px">October 20, 2026 · dshbase · hardware, open gaps, and when the official API is still the right call</p>

  <p>On October 20, 2026, <a href="https://x.com/deepseek_ai/status/2112476496283775135" rel="noopener noreferrer">@deepseek_ai</a> posted a four-part thread introducing <strong>DeepInfer</strong> — DeepSeek’s in-house inference framework — as fully open source, with the code at <a href="https://github.com/deepseek-ai/DeepInfer" rel="noopener noreferrer">github.com/deepseek-ai/DeepInfer</a>. The pitch is blunt: the same stack that powers <strong>DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash</strong> at production scale is now available so the community can chase lower compute cost and energy use.</p>
  <p>If you came here from <a href={homeUrl}>dshbase</a> because you run <code>npx @deepseek-ai/dsh web</code>, this post is not “go clone the repo tonight.” October 20 shipped what community coverage often frames as <strong>two open-source drops</strong> next to the Harness you already use: <strong>DeepInfer</strong> (inference engine) and <strong>deepseek-recipe</strong> (protocols / prompts / tokenization). Confusing any of those three layers is how people burn a weekend and still have no agent loop — or install a codec library expecting GPUs to light up. Below: a fixed three-layer map, verified facts, the hardware gate, what DeepInfer deliberately leaves out, and who should install what.</p>

  <h2>Three layers — keep them distinct</h2>
  <p>Same company brand, <strong>three</strong> jobs. dshbase is about layer 1. The Oct 20 announcement is mainly layers 2 and 3.</p>
  <table class="ztable">
    <tr><th></th><th>1. DeepSeek Harness (DSH)</th><th>2. DeepInfer</th><th>3. deepseek-recipe</th></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Job</strong></td><td>Agent <strong>runtime</strong> / harness (what dshbase documents)</td><td>Production <strong>inference engine</strong> serving V4.1-Flash</td><td>Protocols, prompts, tokenization &amp; codec — Messages / Chat Completions / Responses, etc.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Does inference?</strong></td><td>No — calls a model HTTP API</td><td><strong>Yes</strong> — runs kernels on SM100 / Ascend 950</td><td><strong>No</strong> — caller must bring their own inference backend, tools, and HTTP</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Typical install</strong></td><td><code>npx @deepseek-ai/dsh web</code> + official API key</td><td>Build from source (Python 3.12 + CUDA 13 or CANN 9.2)</td><td>Library / PyPI (<code>deepseek-recipe</code> <strong>0.2.0</strong> uploaded 2026-10-20, same calendar day as the DeepInfer thread)</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Gives you</strong></td><td>Tools, plugins, modes, session audit, agent loop</td><td>Offline / private serving of V4.1-Flash on listed hardware</td><td>API-shaped request→Conversation→prompt→token IDs and response formatting aligned with official behavior</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Does not give you</strong></td><td>A self-hosted Flash cluster or a tokenizer stack</td><td>Agent loops, plugin market, web UI, or a complete multi-tenant “kitchen” (KV cache / PD split / LB still missing — see below)</td><td>Model weights running, GPU scheduling, or DSH plugins</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Verify</strong></td><td><code>@deepseek-ai/dsh</code> / Harness docs</td><td><a href="https://github.com/deepseek-ai/DeepInfer" rel="noopener noreferrer">deepseek-ai/DeepInfer</a></td><td><a href="https://github.com/deepseek-ai/deepseek-recipe" rel="noopener noreferrer">deepseek-ai/deepseek-recipe</a></td></tr>
  </table>
  <p>DeepInfer’s official README: high-performance inference framework for DeepSeek’s large-scale production services; this release “provides <strong>offline inference</strong> with the core features needed for serving at scale.” Supported model at ship time: <strong>DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash</strong> (HF <code>deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash-1017</code>). deepseek-recipe’s README is equally blunt: use it to connect <em>an</em> inference backend to multi-format API fronts — <strong>the caller provides model inference</strong>.</p>
  <p>If your goal is “run an agent on V4.1 Flash,” the default path is still <strong>DSH + official API</strong> (layer 1 only). You do not need DeepInfer or recipe for that.</p>

