  <h2>Hardware and open scope (from the official README)</h2>
  <p>This is the gate most DSH installers will trip on. The README’s supported hardware list is short:</p>
  <table class="ztable">
    <tr><th>Supported hardware (official)</th><th>Prerequisites called out</th></tr>
    <tr><td>NVIDIA <strong>SM100</strong> GPUs</td><td>Linux, GCC ≥ 13, Python 3.12, <strong>CUDA 13</strong></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Huawei <strong>Ascend 950</strong> NPUs</td><td>Same base tooling; <strong>CANN 9.2</strong>; <code>requirements-ascend.txt</code> / <code>DEEPINFER_ACCELERATOR=ascend</code></td></tr>
  </table>
  <p>The README also labels the release as <strong>offline inference</strong>. That matches the community read: useful for shop-floor / private stacks that can assemble the missing pieces, not a drop-in replacement for DeepSeek’s public multi-tenant API.</p>
  <p>Official “required for a complete inference service (<strong>not included</strong> in this release)” checklist — quote the README, do not invent extras:</p>
  <table class="ztable">
    <tr><th>Piece</th><th>In this open release?</th></tr>
    <tr><td>Optimized kernels / model &amp; sampler runtime (FlashMLA, DeepGEMM, DeepEP, TileKernels, DeepSelect, TileLang ops)</td><td><strong>Included</strong> (as documented)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Parallelism: Attention DP / balanced CP, MoE EP + EPLB</td><td><strong>Included</strong></td></tr>
    <tr><td>DSpark + confidence-scheduled verification; SWA bounded replay; async scheduling; batch-invariant / deterministic sampling (NVIDIA notes in README); structured outputs</td><td><strong>Included</strong></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Offline inference example (<code>examples/offline_inference.py</code>)</td><td><strong>Included</strong></td></tr>
    <tr><td>A <strong>KV cache system</strong></td><td><strong>Not included</strong></td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>VE / Prefill / Decode disaggregation</strong> (README: most required engine functionality already implemented, but the disaggregation layer is still listed as required-and-missing)</td><td><strong>Not included</strong></td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Load balancing</strong> across data-parallel replicas for prefill and decode</td><td><strong>Not included</strong></td></tr>
  </table>
  <p>Those three missing pieces are the production “kitchen” around the engine. Community shorthand that DeepInfer is more offline-oriented is consistent with the README’s own wording — cite the README, not rumor throughput.</p>
  <p>DeepSeek publishes Pareto charts under <code>doc/images/</code> for prefill and decode, measured on <strong>4 SM100 GPUs for prefill and 64 for decode</strong> (each within one scale-up domain), with a reproduce script at <code>examples/sweep/run_sweep.sh</code>. Those are <strong>two phases of the same DeepInfer engine</strong> (prefill vs decode), not two products and not a stand-in for deepseek-recipe. We are <strong>not</strong> copying chart tokens-per-second into this article; open the images or re-run the sweep if you need a number for a capacity plan.</p>

  <h2>vs vLLM / SGLang / staying on the official API</h2>
  <p>DeepInfer’s acknowledgments say it was originally built primarily on <a href="https://github.com/vllm-project/vllm" rel="noopener noreferrer">vLLM</a>, with inspiration from <a href="https://github.com/sgl-project/sglang" rel="noopener noreferrer">SGLang</a> and TensorRT-LLM. That is lineage, not a promise that DeepInfer is a general-purpose multi-model server tomorrow.</p>
  <table class="ztable">
    <tr><th>Path</th><th>When it fits a DSH reader</th><th>Barrier (verifiable, not invented $)</th></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Official API + DSH</strong></td><td>Default. You want agents, plugins, modes, and V4.1 Flash behavior without owning GPUs.</td><td>API key + token bill. Set caps — see <a href={costUrl}>cost &amp; caching</a> and the <a href={cachePulseUrl}>token-efficiency pulse</a>.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Self-host DeepInfer</strong></td><td>You already run SM100 or Ascend 950 fleets, need private/offline serving of V4.1-Flash, and can supply KV cache, PD split, and LB yourself.</td><td>Hardware + build (CUDA 13 / CANN 9.2) + the three missing service pieces above. Not a laptop project.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>vLLM / SGLang (community stacks)</strong></td><td>Broader model zoo, more portable hardware stories, or you already operate those stacks.</td><td>May lag Day-0 DeepSeek-specific kernels that DeepInfer ships for Flash; DeepInfer may lag general multi-model ergonomics. Pick from your ops reality, not from a star count.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>deepseek-recipe only (+ your backend)</strong></td><td>Layer 3: custom API front that must match official protocol/prompt/tokenization. Not an agent runtime and not an engine.</td><td>You still provide inference (DeepInfer / vLLM / API), tools, and HTTP — recipe never runs weights by itself.</td></tr>
  </table>
  <p>We invent <strong>no</strong> tok/s, $/MTok, or “N× cheaper than API” claims here. Official marketing language is qualitative (“high throughput,” “low latency,” “lower compute costs”). Capacity math belongs in your own sweep on your own SKUs.</p>

  <h2>Name collision: deepinfer.org is not this project</h2>
  <p>Search engines still surface <a href="http://www.deepinfer.org/" rel="noopener noreferrer">deepinfer.org</a> — an older open-source <strong>medical imaging</strong> deployment toolkit (3D Slicer / Docker model store; SPIE paper lineage). It is unrelated to DeepSeek. When you search, add <strong>DeepSeek</strong> or use the GitHub org path <code>deepseek-ai/DeepInfer</code> so you do not install the wrong binary or cite the wrong paper.</p>

  <h2>DSH reader action checklist</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Stay on DSH + API only (most readers):</strong> keep <code>npx @deepseek-ai/dsh web</code> (or your pinned profile) on the <strong>official DeepSeek API</strong>. You need neither DeepInfer nor deepseek-recipe for day-to-day agents. See <a href={firstDayUrl}>first day with DSH</a> and <a href={modelSetupUrl}>model setup</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>Install the engine (DeepInfer) only if:</strong> you run <strong>SM100 or Ascend 950</strong> shop floors, want private/offline V4.1-Flash serving, and can own KV cache + PD disaggregation + replica LB as separate projects. Start at <a href="https://github.com/deepseek-ai/DeepInfer" rel="noopener noreferrer">deepseek-ai/DeepInfer</a>; read the “not included” list before promising a cutover.</li>
    <li><strong>Need recipe only if:</strong> you are wiring a custom API front (Messages / Chat Completions / Responses) and must match official encode/decode / prompt behavior. Short link: <a href="https://github.com/deepseek-ai/deepseek-recipe" rel="noopener noreferrer">deepseek-ai/deepseek-recipe</a> (PyPI 0.2.0). It does <strong>not</strong> run the model — bring your own backend (DeepInfer, vLLM, official API, …).</li>
    <li><strong>Do not</strong> treat Prefill vs Decode charts, DeepInfer, and deepseek-recipe as three interchangeable downloads. Prefill/Decode are phases inside DeepInfer; recipe is the codec layer; DSH is the agent runtime.</li>
    <li><strong>Do not</strong> download random “DeepInfer one-click” binaries from unofficial mirrors. Prefer the official GitHub trees and Apache-2.0 LICENSE.</li>
    <li><strong>Set token / cost caps</strong> on the agent side regardless of serving path — unbounded tools burn money on API and burn cluster time on self-host. Practical controls: <a href={cachePulseUrl}>token-efficiency note</a> and <a href={costUrl}>cost guide</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>V4.1 Flash context:</strong> prior checkpoint history in our <a href={flashUrl}>V4.1 Flash beta analysis</a>; live model id from current API docs.</li>
    <li><strong>Plugin hygiene:</strong> serving news does not relax plugin risk. Prefer verified entries from the <a href={pluginsUrl}>plugins hub</a> / <a href={directoryUrl}>directory</a>.</li>
  </ul>

